{"timestamp":"00:23:15.190","source":"Browser","level":"LOG","message":"{\"discipline\":\"Doctor\",\"environment\":\"Planning wizard\",\"manual\":\"---\\n## 1. Title\\n\\n**Installing Visual Studio Code on RHEL, Fedora, and CentOS-based Distributions Using the Planning Wizard**\\n\\n---\\n## 2. Purpose\\n\\nThis work instruction provides step-by-step guidance for installing Visual Studio Code on RHEL, Fedora, and CentOS-based Linux distributions using terminal commands and the Planning wizard interface. The instructions ensure the correct addition of the required Microsoft repository and the installation of the VS Code package.\\n\\n---\\n## 3. Prerequisites\\n\\n- Access to a terminal on a supported RHEL, Fedora, or CentOS-based distribution.\\n- Sufficient privileges to execute `sudo` commands.\\n- An active internet connection.\\n- The `dnf` package manager must be available (for Fedora 22 and later).\\n- If `sudo` is not available or not recognized, you may require direct root access.\\n\\n---\\n## 4. Procedure\\n\\n### Step 1: Install the Microsoft Key and Yum Repository\\n\\n1. Open a terminal session.\\n2. Enter the following command to import the Microsoft GPG key and set up the yum repository (as shown in the Planning wizard):\\n    ```bash\\n    sudo rpm --import https://packages.microsoft.com/keys/microsoft.asc && \\\\\\n    echo -e \\\"[code]\\\\nname=Visual Studio Code\\\\nbaseurl=https://packages.microsoft.com/yumrepos/vscode\\\\nenabled=1\\\\nautorefresh=1\\\\ntype=rpm-md\\\\ngpgcheck=1\\\\ngpgkey=https://packages.microsoft.com/keys/microsoft.asc\\\" | sudo tee /etc/yum.repos.d/vscode.repo > /dev/null\\n    ```\\n   - **Note:** If you receive an error stating `sudo: command not found`, root access is required. Enter the commands as the root user.\\n\\n### Step 2: Update the Package Cache and Install Visual Studio Code\\n\\n1. Run the following commands to update your package cache and install Visual Studio Code:\\n    ```bash\\n    dnf check-update &&\\n    sudo dnf install code\\n    ```\\n    - **Note:** If `sudo` is not available, use the root shell to run the commands.\\n\\n2. The terminal will display progress for loading repositories and list any available package upgrades. On successful completion, Visual Studio Code will be installed.\\n\\n---\\n## 5. Tips\\n\\n- Ensure you use the terminal connected to the correct server or workstation.\\n- The command sequence and repository details must match those provided to avoid installation issues.\\n- If `dnf` is not recognized or you are using an older distribution, the required alternative steps (such as using `yum`) cannot be determined from the provided information.\\n\\n---\\n## 6. FAQ\\n\\n**Q1: What if the command `sudo` is not found?**  \\nA1: As seen in the terminal screenshot, if `sudo` is unavailable, you must switch to a root shell before running the provided commands.\\n\\n**Q2: What repository do these steps add?**  \\nA2: These steps add the Microsoft repository for Visual Studio Code, enabling package management through your system’s default package manager.\\n\\n**Q3: Are there alternative instructions for older distributions using `yum`?**  \\nA3: The screenshots reference usage of `yum` for older distributions, but the specific commands are not visible or provided.\\n\\n**Q4: Is it possible to install Code Insiders?**  \\nA4: The screenshot indicates that `code-insiders` may be available, but the command or process for that cannot be determined from the images provided.\\n\\n---\\n\\n*If you need to document further actions (such as launching Visual Studio Code), the required details are not available in the screenshots.*\",\"screenshots\":[\"Screenshot From 2026-07-06 19-26-23.png\",\"Screenshot From 2026-07-06 19-30-30.png\"],\"status\":\"success\"}"}
